  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\ndiminishing winds and clouds.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft between low pressure departing the Canadian\nMaritimes and high pressure approaching from the Great Lakes\nwill maintain the status quo through Sunday evening. Waves of\nstratocumulus along with a few sprinkles or flurries are\nanticipated east of the Appalachians with a moisture fetch off\nthe Great Lakes, with intermittent snow showers along and west \nof the Allegheny Front through Sunday. Little to no measurable \nprecipitation is expected for the majority of the area, but a \ndusting of snow is possible for west-facing slopes above 1900 \nfeet along and west of the Allegheny Front, with spotty 1 to 2 \ninch amounts possible above about 2700 feet.\n\nA few snow/sleet pellets or snizzle/very spotty light freezing\ndrizzle/fog can't be ruled out over the highest peaks along and\nwest of the Allegheny Front due to shallower moisture at times.\n\nOf note, the 14/12Z REFS had a few members with several\nhundredths of an inch of QPF from a streamer over the eastern WV\npanhandle, north-central MD and into extreme northern VA tonight\nthrough Sunday. We do not currently have PoPs for measurable \nprecipitation in these areas (just the mention of scattered \nsprinkles and flurries), but can't totally discount the idea of\na steadier shower (or perhaps a light dusting of snow above \nabout 700-800 feet) through Sunday evening.\n\nAfter peaking this afternoon, wind gusts will gradually subside\nthrough the remainder of the weekend into early next week. Gusts\nwill be slower to subside at higher elevations (especially at\nnight compared to the lower elevations). Clouds will also\ngradually thin and scatter out during this period.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist through next week with \nincreasing temperatures starting Tuesday.\n\nWinds continue to diminish on Tuesday and remain light through \nthe end of the week as high pressure transitions overhead in the\nwake of the front. As upper-level ridging continues to build \nover several days, dry conditions are expected to persist \nthrough the end of the forecast period with mostly clear skies \noverhead and progressively warming temperatures as the week goes\non. Afternoon highs could begin to approach the upper 60s \nbetween Thursday and Friday, well above seasonable normals, with\novernight lows generally in the 40s. No precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated Tuesday through Friday.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of next week, an upper-level trough \ncould develop over the Great Lakes and produce a stemming cold \nfront in the days following; will continue to monitor this \npattern for any precipitation potential over the coming days.",
